There are just so many things to love about WordPress. It’s easy to use, free, flexible and is backed by a large community that’s always willing to help beginners out.
When it comes to usability, one of the most important tools you need in your WordPress arsenal is a form builder. It allows your audience to interact with your brand – be it in the shape of general inquiries, support requests, or feedback.
Of course, using a template is a huge time-saver, especially if you already know what you’re trying to accomplish with your form. But for the sake of demonstrating the features of Google Forms, let’s go ahead and start from a blank template.
Currently, Google Forms supports the following question types for collecting different information.
You can use as many questions as you like in a single form. To do this, click the ‘Add question' button in the floating sidebar.
As a rule of thumb, only ask for the information you need from your target users. If you ask too many questions or become too invasive, users might be discouraged to complete their submission.
Once copied, go to your WordPress dashboard and open the page or post where you want the form to appear. Switch to the text editor to view the HTML code for your content.
Alternatively, you can simply switch back to the visual editor to check if the form displays correctly.
If your only goal is to embed Google Forms to your WordPress site, then the answer is yes. However, wpGForm does a lot more than just integrating forms.
To access this, click the ‘Help & Feedback' button and select ‘Back to the old Forms.'
Next, paste the link to your form into the “Form URL” field. You can obtain this by going to the Google Forms editor and clicking ‘File' > ‘Send form.'
If a valid URL has been inserted, your form is now ready for use via shortcode. This can be accessed by going to ‘Google Forms' > ‘Google Forms.'
But in the right hands, Google Forms and wpGForm is a powerful combination that can take your data collection efforts to the next level. You can define CSS rules, setup input validation, enable hidden fields, and override the default text in Google Form buttons.
