49 Data Proven Tips On How To Get More Retweets
To go viral in the Twitter universe, you need a sound tactic.
The following list covers some of the most important elements in my Twitter success. If more retweets is all you want, I am sure you'll find it valuable.
Use call for action in your tweets. Phrases like ‘Please retweet', ‘RT' and ‘pls RT' are proven words that get you more retweets.
State your retweet/follow back policy clearly on your profile to encourage interaction. See how Ted Coine does it here.
Better still, schedule and send out the same tweets in various time in a day or week. For example, Tim Ferris posts the same tweet 12 hours after the first in order to reach followers from different time zone.
Show that there's a real human behind the monitor, put up your personal photo on your personal Twitter account.
Don't just tweet the title of an article, add value to your tweets with your personal comment or summary of the subject.
Find the top 20 most retweeted words in your industry, use them more frequently in your tweets.
Talk less about mundane activities. We are not your mom, no one is interested on what you are having for lunch.
Use TwitPic. Most people are visual-oriented hence pictures tend to get more attention from the crowd.
Get more exposures, add yourself at Twellow.
I do shout out for my followers regularly, something like “Please follow @userA @userB @userC etc” and it always bring back more follows and retweets. People normally tend to reciprocate others' goodwill, as the old saying give and you shall receive.
Run a Twitter contest and ask the contestants to retweet your stuff (WebRevenue.co might be interested to be one of your sponsors, let's talk).
Stop talking about yourself. There are only 1% of the retweets contain self-reference according to studies.
Learn from someone who has done it, model his/her method. Tip: read Gina's 5 Easy Steps To Get 10,000 Followers.
Over To You: Tell Us Your Retweet Tips!
I look forward to your input.
Home › Blog › Hosting Guides › Domain Names for UK Residents Operating a Global Business
When you’re running a global business, it can be challenging to know which domain name is right for you or best represents your presence online. Even when you’re operating or headquartered in one place, like the UK, you may have a shop that ships products across Europe or a company with satellite offices around the world.
1. What is your company or brand name?
If your brand name is completely different from your domain name, this could impact your searchability online. Make sure to secure a domain name that is closely tied to your company’s name.
Your location can impact your domain name if you choose to get a Country-Code Top-Level Domain (ccTLD) that represents your company’s region. This will assist your success in ranking in local searches based on the country or region you own the domain name for.
Your customers may be across the world and want to find your products or services. Owning a domain name extension for their location may be an intelligent decision to build brand trust and assist in search, as with the above location-based ccTLD advice.
They can be fun to use as your main domain name or to forward to your main domain. For example, your main brand may be helensdanceyoga.uk, and you may also own helensdanceyoga.fit and helensdance.yoga to cover all your bases and protect your brand name.
Country Code Top-Level Domains for Business Outside UK If you’re based out of the UK and on the domain name hunt, there are plenty of Country Code Top-Level Domains (ccTLDs) that can reflect your location, your global-reach, or your industry such as the following:
The .IO domain name is hugely popular amongst the startup and tech communities. Despite being a ccTLD, it’s often associated with the “Input/Output” terminology, which is why it has been so popular in the tech and startup scene.
The .CO ccTLD has taken the world by storm. Almost like a short version of .COM, companies, corporations, and communities like this domain name extension to represent what they do.
If you’re headquartered in the UK, a place that is home to over 5.6 million small businesses as of 2018, why not grab the domain name to represent where it all began?
With over five million brands and individuals using the .INFO extension, it’s a trusted domain name to put your brand on.
The .CHARITY domain name extension is a new one, released in 2018, which means the odds of finding the exact one you want is high. Having a .CHARITY domain name is great for your charitable organization.
Launching your new LLC is an exciting time!
There are plenty of interesting words that have been made into Top-Level Domains to help represent all verticals and industries. Don’t feel discouraged when your first choice domain name is taken or if you won’t be able to maintain your .EU domain.
How Carol Tice Makes A Living From Her Freelance Writing Blog
“Don’t you need to have hundreds of thousands of email subscribers and get millions of pageviews to make that kind of money?”
Carol Tice started her second stint as a freelance writer back in 2005, having also freelanced for 5 years or so back in the ‘90s. Most people who go down that road end up overworked and underpaid.
She didn’t start her blog for the money – she started it because she was infuriated by the exploitation of writers that she saw all around her.
I thought maybe I could help other writers — no, that’s wrong. I felt compelled to speak out against something I saw as flat-out evil and to help writers find better pay,” explains Carol in her Write to Done article.
And so, in 2008, Make a Living Writing was born. Who could have predicted that a blog in an already competitive niche would go on to become a $500,000+/year business?
People often make the mistake of doing the market analysis, identifying a profitable niche, and just going into it, without any regard to whether they themselves are interested in the topic. This works on paper, but the reality is that in order to build a profitable blog you need to produce quality content on a regular basis, and you probably won’t be able to do it if you don’t care about the topic.
If you see your blog as a hobby, then passion is all you need. However, if you want to build a business, you also need to evaluate a niche’s earning potential.
That means you can make money in it, too.
There will be established players in any profitable niche, but that doesn’t mean you can’t carve out a place for yourself. Stay focused, stay patient, and one day you’ll become an A-lister yourself.
Carol took her blog seriously from the very beginning. She would put her kids to sleep at 8, and then work on her blog until midnight.
Then there was the business side. Any chance I saw to grab a free training online, read a blog post, or talk to an expert who could teach me something about blogging success, I did it.
Carol studied blog headline writing, worked hard on her posts, and soon attracted the interest of then-Copyblogger associate editor Jon Morrow, and Derek Halpern of Social Triggers. These and other mentors coached her on blogging best practices, introduced her around and helped her guest post for top blogs.
However, only a handful of those visitors converted to subscribers, which made her seek out help.
Then, one day, Mary from A-List Blogging emailed her and suggested that she enter Write to Done’s Top 10 Blogs for Writers contest. Carol didn’t expect much, but figured it wouldn't hurt to try.
That tweet from Jon Morrow was one of Carol’s lucky breaks, but what role did luck really play in it?
What do you have to lose? Worst-case scenario, it ends up being a flop, but then you’ll still have learned something from it.
After winning the Write to Done contest, Carol went from ~300 email subscribers to ~1,000 email subscribers, and that allowed her to start making some money from her blog.
She did surveys that helped her understand the acutest pain points of her audience. The answers surprised me.
Learning about my readers’ challenges helped me write more useful posts. I started doing regular mailbag posts, where I’d reprint questions readers had emailed me and answer them.
Lessons learned Overcome your reluctance to sell
Then you need to find something or someone that will help you get your mind straight. It can be free content, it can be premium material, it can be advice from an entrepreneurial friend… Whatever works for you!
You might think that you know what your readers want, but chances are you don’t.
Over the years, she has released affordable ebooks, webinars, and writing courses, but the most important thing was turning her membership site into a thriving community. At the moment, The Den membership costs $25/month, and it has more than 1, 100 paying members.
In her Writers in Charge interview, she recommends starting with a product that is easy to create, price it at $0.99 – $2.99, and make the pitch to your audience. Then, create a second product that is a bit more valuable and a bit more expensive.
According to Carol, this will work much better for a niche blogger with a small email list than launching an expensive product right off the bat.
It all goes back to building relationships with your readers, listening to them, and giving them what they want.”
Prices: Start low, move up
It’s because the price is affordable, yet the product itself is incredibly valuable. That leaves people feeling like it’s a bargain and keeps them coming back for more.
You might be a bit skeptical of Carol’s advice because she started Make a Living Writing when the blogosphere was much less crowded and competitive than it is now. Is it still possible to build a six-figure blog if you start now?
You are probably not going to break the $500,000/year mark. But you probably could build a six-figure blog if you put your mind to it.
Who knows, maybe 10 years from now, you’ll be an A-lister in your niche, and other people will be intimidated by your success!
