Website builders don’t have nearly as many plugins or themes as WordPress. BUT, because all their add-ons are built in house, most of them will work just fine without you having to tweak them.
To use WordPress you need to find a web host that can install WordPress on MySQL and with PHP support. You may also need to learn how to use an ftp or find another way to upload WordPress files to your host.
Most good hosts will also offer you domain specific emails, but most website builders don’t.
Most website builders will have some limitations, such as a maximum amount of traffic (bandwidth), even with their most expensive options. Some may even display their ads on your site with a low cost paid plan.
8- Popularity
WordPress on the other hand has been around for over 14 years. It’s used in more than 27.5% of the top 10 million websites, and almost 60% of all the websites there are.
If you run into trouble with WordPress, you can easily Google for an answer. With a site builder, your best hope is the company has a decent guide or help pages.
We promised we’d give you a definite answer, and we will. But first we’ll just sum up when it makes sense to use each.
A website builder makes sense if you:
Don’t need email addresses or are willing to pay a different service for email
WordPress makes sense if you:
Want more control over the final look and feature set of your website
Need functionality a website builder cannot provide
Want control over the hosting
So What Do We Think?
In the end though, don’t let decision paralysis get to you— sometimes you just need to get your hands dirty and try something. We recommend you select the best web host that offers both WordPress support and a free website builder.
