Home › Blog › Web Tools › 26 Free Site Builders to Create Amazing Websites
But I’m sure you’ve heard this advice before: If you’re serious about your online presence, DON’T use a FREE website builder!
Also, you certainly don’t look professional with a website with a URL like:
Yet, in this post, I’m asking you to build a website using a free website builder.
Website building tools and extensions: About $60 – 120/year (these could add up to quite a good sum)
An annual managed WordPress-hosting (costing somewhere at $48)
But what if you realized after two weeks that WordPress — no matter how great it looked in the beginning — WASN’T for you, and that you had just lost 200 bucks?
Now WordPress is a great CMS and I use it for most of my websites.
It’s generally difficult to be sure of a website platform before actually building a website with it.
All you need to do is give yourself some time with the different platforms to find out the one that’s perfect for you.
Read up on their descriptions and shortlist the 3 you think would be the most suitable for you.
The difference between the two is that WordPress.com is a hosted solution where Automattic (the parent company of WordPress) takes care of the hosting, upgrades, security, and maintenance of your site, whereas in WordPress.org, you need to arrange the hosting and do all the maintenance work yourself.
You can use WordPress.com’s free version to start a blog or website. What I like the most about WordPress.com is the simplicity that it brings back to blogging and publishing.
Plus, it comes with the famous WordPress blog editor, so if writing is your focus and you don’t want to do the technical stuff with WordPress.org, then WordPress.com is the way for you.
With WordPress.com, you can build any kind of a website. Whether it’s a savvy business website or a simple blog.
Nor does WordPress come with drag and drop page builders with different design modules. Basically, you just get what your theme offers, hence choose carefully.
Wix is one of our favorite online website maker tools. It powers millions of websites around the world.
Basically, this Artificial Design Intelligence site builder virtually builds your site for you once you tell it what your site is about. This is a highly recommended feature.
So is Wix the right choice for you?
Also, if you contrast Wix to some other premium website maker online tools, you’ll realize that it offers more templates, so if you feel bored, you don’t have to pull out your hair in frustration. Simply switch a template, and there are enough templates!
Like I said above, going with Weebly gives you the security of a solid company that won’t just shut down its store one day!
Weebly’s free version gives you 500MB of storage and access to all the templates as well as the drag and drop editor. You also get access to Weebly’s SEO and lead capture features.
In most cases, yes. But if you care about SEO, then the least you should expect to pay with Weebly is $25/mo because to get the SEO-friendly SSL certificate, this is the plan you’ll need.
Ucraft is an awesome website maker tool that lets you build elegant personal websites. It comes with a cool logo maker app where you can use it for free to create and download a high-resolution file.
The most interesting feature of Ucraft’s free version is that it lets you add a custom domain name. I think Ucraft is one of the only two free website builders that allow you to connect a custom domain name on a free plan.
If a one-page website is all you need, then don’t look further than Ucraft because it’s free in the real sense!
I like the idea of a one-page website … and more than websites, Carrd creates cover pages that you can use to tell your story beautifully. Right now, Carrd has about 18 templates, of which about 6 are part of the premium plan.
So is Carrd the right choice for you?
For roughly $1.50 a month, Carrd gives you a beautiful, mobile-friendly one-page website, so keep an eye for this one!
One of the first things I look for in a site builder is its templates. And it becomes very tough to judge such a tool when you can’t see its templates without signing up!
SiteBuilder not only gives great looking templates but its pro plan (see details below) is also thoughtful of someone with a limited budget. So if you want a custom domain name and email and hosting within budget, it’s tough to beat this one at this price point.
While Yola has limited templates, they’re good for basic business/professional websites.
Webs comes with some very diverse and good themes. I just wish more of them were unlocked even in the free plan.
Which is quite strange!
CV, team, slideshows, text, people – you name it. All you need to do is drag and drop these elements into your website.
The complete IM Creator solution is free for nonprofits and students. So, if you’re still studying or are super-passionate about a cause, give IM Creator a try today.
Thanks to its diverse templates and modules, IM Creator’s is quite a catch even for its premium plan.
Clearly, it’s the collection of templates that gives Sitey the upper hand. The templates along with with the tool’s drag and drop page maker tool covers all your needs.
So is Sitey the right choice for you?
Jimdo has limited but beautiful templates. It also has a website showcase section, and if you check it out, you’ll see that Jimdo customers have built some really sleek and creative websites.
So is Jimdo the right choice for you?
If you check out Jimdo’s reviews, you’ll notice a lot of people raving about Jimdo’s eCommerce functionality. So if you want to open an online store, then maybe Jimdo is even more suitable for you.
Site 123 has a no-nonsense pricing scheme. This is great because as you can see in this list, many website builders force cheaper plans that are plain useless.
Site 123 can even be used to build sleek one-page websites, which can be an awesome feature if you want to build a portfolio website.
So is Site 123 the right choice for you?
Till date, WebStarts powers about 3.8 million websites, so you can say it’s a company you can trust.
Despite how the homepage looks, WebStart’s templates are actually nice!
The free plan here feels pretty restrictive despite the generous storage because it doesn’t let you add a contact form to your site. So I don’t know how such a website could benefit you.
A good use case for this website maker tool are business websites that need zero tweaking and can pay up to $30.98/mo because this plan includes advanced features like an email marketing suite, a lead management solution, a CRM, and CDN. Otherwise, you’ve better alternatives within the same budget.
Webnode has some great looking themes. Its higher premium plans offer membership registrations, so if you ever need to let people create accounts on your website, you can unlock the membership registration feature with Webnode’s $19.95/mo plan.
Doodlekit’s free version lets you build a full website with no restriction as such on the number of pages. Storage wise, you get 100MB, which is ok.
I think you really need to check out the templates to decide if Doodlekit is for you, because at Doodlekit’s premium price point, you have alternatives that offer slick templates and even drag and drop editors.
I like Cabanova’s website, so I believe its templates might be good as well, but it’s difficult to tell without looking at them.
The free version lets you build a 3-page website and gives 50MB of data. And from what it seems, the free version unlocks all the templates.
Cabanova looks like a good and cost-effective option but I think I’d say this more confidently if I got the chance to look at all the templates because unlike a few of the online website maker tools on this list, Cabanova doesn’t have a drag and drop editor, which means finding the perfect theme will make all the difference in this case.
This 4-year old website maker tool aims to help small businesses create and maintain their online presence in a hasslefree manner.c
Bookmark Bookmark is built with the purpose of empowering even non-technical people to make beautiful and fully functional websites from scratch.
Signing up for Bookmark unlocks Bookmark’s e-Learning courses as well where Bookmark teaches you how to take your business online and give few tips on how to grow it further.
Siteleo looks like a solid website and its templates too are stunning.
Honestly, I’m absolutely floored by the 10,000 themes you get with Sitelio. All Sitelio’s themes spanning across categories like business, blog, photography, wedding and more are beautiful.
While Sitem.co is still in Beta, it hosts the websites of 247 proud owners!
The thing that I like best about Sitem is that it has simple yet blazing fast themes. And because most of Sitem users are expected to use it for showcasing their work, these elegant templates solve the purpose perfectly.
The free version supports SSL, so search engines will perceive your site to be more trustworthy. Also, it’s ad-free, so Sitem won’t stuff ads into your website and you’ll be able to offer a clutter-free experience to your visitors.
If the main purpose of your website is (and will be) just to showcase your work, then you shouldn't look beyond Sitem.
Plus, at this price point, this is a great option to showcase your work for a lifetime.
Also, when you sign up for a uCoz account, you are automatically signed up for its sister service called uID, which again is not quite a great thing. (In case you're wondering, uID is a service like ‘About me’)
uCoz’s free version is also ad-free. So is uCoz the right choice for you?
If you’re a freelancer, no matter what services you offer, Tilda has beautiful templates to cover all your needs. You can also use it to create special services pages to promote specific services.
While this platform’s focus is primarily on displaying work, it also supports pages like the blog and store, so you can extend your portfolio into a full-fledged website if you want to.
Also, Portfolio’s free version doesn’t stuff ads in your portfolio.
Duda offers an excellent free website builder tool. And given that Duda is all about responsive websites, you can be sure that even your free website will render beautifully on all mobile and tablet devices.
Template wise too, I think Duda has many inspiring designs. Plus, you also get Duda’s drag and drop editor.
Disha Sharma is a digital marketer-turned-freelance writer. She writes about SEO, email and content marketing, and lead generation.
Your Complete Guide for Engaging Brands, Reps and Bloggers | WHSR
Home › Blog › Blogging Tips › Your Complete Guide for Engaging Brands, Reps and Bloggers
Now that you’re blogging, it’s time to get professional about your networking. There are a multitude of opportunities to connect with like-minded bloggers, brands that fit your niche and products that suite your audience.
There are essentially 3 types of venues: conferences, expos and niched events. Each has a different feel.
These are training events meant to draw people from a particular niche or interested in a common subject and they tend to pricy. There are plenty of conferences for bloggers.
Expos are events where brands gather to showcase their products to the general public or conference attendees and offer samples, coupons and/or swag to visitors.
Some coincide with large conferences and most are invitation only. How do you get invited?
Last winter, another blogger invited me to carpool to the Family Game Summit event. It showcased dozens of Activision’s latest games as well as information on safety for kids gaming online.
You can also bundle your event with that vacation you've been waiting to take, to save time and money.
While it can be overwhelming to go to a large expo, I recommend that you search out the top 3-5 sponsors that you would love to work with and get to know them very well by following them. Research them in great detail in press and in their feeds, and look for relative news that might affect them, especially if it plays into your niche.
Every week, I write about food dangers and toxins in our environment every week, researching the facts from the most reliable sources I can find to get to the bottom of controversies and creating pinnable images for my job at Mamavation. My best posts have gone viral.
Attend early. You can stop at every single table, but if you hit your top picks first, you can get to them before too many other bloggers have.
You probably won’t hand out the kit, but just bring it just in case you are asked.
Don’t hassle them.
Some of my favorite green bloggers, who I met in 2010 and I'm still friends with.
For bloggers, most of the above applies, but with a more casual approach. In addition, these tips will help:
Briedly tell the blogger what you’re all about and then listen to their story and ask questions. You're more likely to find common ground that way – or learn that you’re not a good fit.
Can they guest post on your blog?
Just like with brands, after you get home, share and favorite their social media posts and comment on their blogs. 4.
This means helping bloggers bigger than you AND reaching out to new or small bloggers. You will build friendships as well as professional relationships.
Keep in touch, keep promoting them and keep saying “hi”. Attend their Twitter parties.
Tag them with things you know they’d love.
You might want to build a G+ community and create a Twitter list for bloggers you’ve met at conferences, and do connect again in person at the next conference!
One way to remember faces is to snap a photo with you, the rep and their product. I did this at Blogger Bash to remember who was at what table in the expo.
You had a great conversation with this person – but you also had 20 other great conversations. How can you remember the discussion?
If there’s no room, you might want to bring small, white stickers to stick over unessential information for notes.
Separate or code business cards from bloggers, brands and reps. if you’re going to multiple events at the same time, sort events. One blogger I met brought a small hole punch and put the cards on individuals key rings labeled with event name.
That is everything you need to network like a pro when you attend events. Share your creative ideas for keeping organized at events.
Things We Wish We Knew Before Starting A Blog | WHSR
Things We Wish We Knew Before Starting A Blog
If you could turn back time, what are the things you would teach yourself before starting a blog?
My Biggest Mistake: Not Building an Email List
I never bother to start a newsletter program or email marketing campaign or whatsoever throughout the past 6 years. I thought email is for MLM marketers; we geeks hack growth via SEO and smart SMM tactics.
Email was just not going to be my thing. (Email is still not likely my thing at this point of writing but at least I am learning and trying nowadays.)
A big, fat, email list that I would have built during the good 2,500-visitors-per-day time. Things could turn out very different.
Email is by far the most effective way of directly growing your business
Fortunately, that’s the only problem because when it comes to comparing them as marketing vehicles and return-on-investment, Email is by far the most effective way of directly affecting your bottom line and actually growing your business.
And I am not alone.
We didn't collect our blog visitors' email, big mistake – Ashli
For one, we didn't collect our blog visitors' email.
My #1 blogging mistake –not building an email list right from day 1
I mean, you spend all that time and effort perfecting your blog posts, being social on social media, and marketing your blog – but what happens once you've hooked a new reader?
The way I use email marketing in top10zen.com is by constantly updating my readers about new content on the website. I make sure not to send too many emails, and only when I know they'll be interested.
By the way, I use Mailchimp for email marketing. Their free version is good for up to 2000 subscribers.
So how do you start building your email list?
3. Create an awesome signup form and put it up on your blog
And hola, you are set to start collecting emails.
Other blogging mistakes we wish to avoid
To everyone that participated in this survey: A huge, huge THANK YOU!
Be consistent, don't do it all by yourself, and help others – Devesh Sharma
If you want to become a successful blogger, you have to be original and consistence with your blogging routine.
But I never spend any money on content and website design, I did it all the work by myself. But now that I look back, I think I would have made a lot more money, if I had spend just 20% of what I was earning back on the site.
Another point I would like to add that, it is always good to focus on your strengths, instead of trying to do all by yourself, you should focus on doing what you are good at.
Helping other People Blogging is not all about money, when I first started I focused on generating traffic and money.
Perfection is a bloggers best friend and worst enemy – I’m a perfectionist by nature, that’s how I’ve always been and there are elements of that which can be an amazing asset. It can provide a path to creating a ground breaking blog, but the downside is that it can also paralyze you and stop you from progressing at all.
P/S: Adam just published a similar post recently– 15 things I wish I had known before I started blogging – a very good read in my opinion. Go check out.
It takes a while before anyone reads or comments on your blog. It's just the nature of the internet that less than 0.5 percent comment.The important thing is to keep posting quality content.
My biggest mistakes when starting my blog were:
Choosing a uncommon, hard to remember or spell domain name. It was bad for growing an audience.
