Home › Blog › WordPress › W3 Total Cache Plugin – Beyond General Settings
In anearlier article, we stressed that it is important to have a fast loading website and that W3 Total Cache plugin can help reduce load time and generally speed up websites. We also looked at the General Settings for this plugin in that article.
After you install and activate W3 Total Cache plugin, look forPerformanceon the dashboard and click on it. The Dashboard of the plugin will open up and you can browse there to have an idea of the range of functions of this plugin.
In the image above, the preview mode is disabled. You must enable the preview mode while making changes to settings, observe that they have the desired effect on the website and then disable them again after saving the changes.
Every time a viewer calls for a page, WordPress accesses numerous PHP scripts and runs database queries, all of which take up server space and slow down the website. When you enable caching on a page, this process is made quicker for repeated requests.
This is fine for most websites. As you will have to make this choice repeatedly for all the settings, let's take a look at the other options besides Disk Enhanced.
Dedicated/Virtual Servers are a higher order of servers. Dedicated servers are not shared with any other website or person, while Virtual servers are shared by very few persons or websites.
Enabling the objects cache helps reduce the process time of some operations. It is advisable only for Dedicated or Virtual Server.
Browser cache is a very important option and must always be enabled, whatever hosting method you are using. This setting uses the cache in the web browser of the visitor to reduce server load and response time.
Enable only when required and disable soon after. All settings can be exported and then imported subsequently into the same site or a different location and serves as a good backup.
Home › Blog › Hosting Guides › The Curious Case of Absolutely Free Domain Name
With more than 348 million domain names registered as of end 2018, domain names are hot selling commodities. In fact, there has been such great demand that the Internet Corporation for Assigned Names and Numbers (ICANN) has been allowing the registration of new Top-Level Domains.
Domain names that are cheap to register often come with a catch though – the renewal fees are often much higher than what you bought them for. Take for example the case of 1&1 Ionos which lets you register a .info domain for $1 but charges $20 for subsequent renewals.
Domain names are priced differently depending on exactly what TLD is involved. Essentially, the amount you pay is split up between three main bodies –
Domain name registries do not directly sell domain names but allow other companies called registrars to handle these matters. For example, in the case of a .com TLD, although Verisign is the registry you would buy the actual domain name from a registrar like NameCheap.
Finally, we come to Internet Corporation for Assigned Names and Numbers (ICANN) which is the top dog in the domain name business. Because ICANN is a non-profit organization, it only charges a nominal fee of 18 cents (per year) for the sale of each domain name which falls under its governance.
Well, ICANN is not the only body which oversees domain names.
Its only other apparent service is a public DNS system (similar to the ones Google or Cloudflare operate). You can search for the domain name you want and the Freenom system will display a list of what’s available (or not) and for what price.
Aside from the free (and shady) .tk domains you also might be able to get a free domain name from one of the web hosting providers. This is a much better option, since in many cases the domains they are giving away are more reputable extensions.
This is available even on their shared hosting plans like the Premium and Business plans. For a fee of only $2.15 and $3.45 respectively per month, both come with a free domain name registration.
As far back as in 2007, Internet Security giant McAfee found that a stunning 10% of all .tk domains contained malware. However, since then there have been other ccTLDs emerging which have even worse reputations, for example .cm, .cn and .ws.
On the other hand, buying a good web hosting package from Hostinger may involve some cost for the hosting deal – but you need web hosting anyway, right?
