5 Reasons Why Content Marketing Will Always Triumph Link Building | WHSR
Perhaps both?
But let’s face it: the SEO land is actually divided into two schools of thought, whether to stick with traditional SEO approach using keyword-marketing and link building, or pursue content marketing that includes several tasks.
So, which is which?
Randy Fishkin of SEOmoz explained these two ‘lands’—link building land and content marketing land—previously in a Whiteboard Friday episode. It’s a thought-provoking strategy discussion among webmasters and marketers, where there will always be two sides of the camp.
Our premise to why content marketing will always triumph over link building doesn’t end there. It’s not a question of “which is which,” but rather “what’s your goal?
In a nutshell, link building is the technical side of web marketing, where SEO specialists’ tasks involve this triple-braided cycle: keyword targeting, finding list of link opportunities, and getting links.
On the other hand, the content marketing land is tedious, daunting, and complex that it requires you to hire not just the SEO specialists, but also writers, community managers, copywriters or if you’re in luck, a web marketer who knows the ropes of the trade in content writing, copywriting, and social media.
2. Quality content is link-able and likable
Search Engine Watch said that you could measure the value of content easier than you can link.
It’s pretty simple. When you post content, you can see how many links and organic search traffic it has generated—the number of social media shares and referral visits in the analytics dashboard.
Shareable and sustainable Coming from the expert, Randy Fishkin said that just a single piece of content or a couple every year or two can be everything you need to make content marketing strategy sustainable for long-term.
For us, it’s all about satisfying our customers and providing educational quality content that solves their daily concerns and questions about the products and services they want to purchase. With content marketing, you can be as generous as you can on your posts.
Are they Facebook-savvy customers?
Are they a bunch of readers and intellects, hungry for data and useful information?
Content marketing includes investing on content that’s well researched and written that could require a good copywriter days to create. Most importantly, it’s clear and directly solves your customers’ concerns.
If we’re talking about long-term goals and building brand’s authority, definitely!
But remember, the process of content marketing itself is laborious and requires lots of efforts and focus compared with link building, yet it’s one of the best investments in online marketing. What do you think of these two schools of thought in the SEO World?
