Every action we take online is gathered, analyzed, bought, and exploited every day.
And your blog opens you up to more risk than the average internet user – which is why your online safety and privacy should be a major concern.
As a blogger, you’re risking:
You might think that your blog is an unlikely target, but consider the following common motivations for attackers:
Vandalism: Your site could be targeted just for the fun or challenge of it.
Competition: A competitor might decide to use underhanded tactics to level the playing field.
2- Your Finances
Or, they may have enough info on your identity to open up credit in your name, max it out, and leave you to take the hit on your credit report when the debt goes unpaid.
If you blog anonymously, someone could piece together your identity and use it against you.
While HIPAA laws may not apply to you, many countries and industries have their own laws on privacy, so mentioning details about your work could land you in trouble.
At the other end of the spectrum, your own personal safety – and even your life – could be at risk.
Harassment, even if it’s contained to the Internet and not “real life,” is no joke and can have serious emotional and psychological effects on the victim.
Believe it or not, I was the victim of harassment by my own sister. I won't go into the details of why she did it, but she had her friends from an MS board post truly awful comments on my blog because she was angry with me.
Blogging puts you at risk from harassers who not only will ruin the experience of blogging, but could also put your safety at risk.
Negative posts written about me on another blog] were enough to trigger some extreme thought processes and obsessions in this person that led to a chain of escalating events that went from what I initially considered to be a harmless comment troll, to a cyber-nuisance, to a concerning threat maker, to what unfortunately became a situation where there was a physical attack made upon my property.
Six Ways to Secure Your Personal Data as a Blogger
2- Use domain privacy
Please share in the comments!)
Bottom Line: Keep Safety in Mind When Blogging!
12 Step-by-Step Points to Write Your First Online Course – Part I | WHSR
Home › Blog › Copy Writing › 12 Step-by-Step Points to Write Your First Online Course – Part I
Back in 1996, I started offering online writing courses through chat rooms to a handful of students. At that time, there weren't as many online course options out there and what I and others were doing was still groundbreaking.
This article is in two parts. The first part of this article will go through everything you need to know in order to get your first course up and running, from idea to promotion.
Your first step in creating an online course is to choose a topic to teach. This should ideally be:
If you are going to teach others, you need to understand the topic inside and out. Imagine if you were standing in front of a room full of people and they were asking you questions about this topic.
Now, try to find holes in those courses. Are there topics that are not completely explained or skipped over?
What extra can you offer?
By packaging the topic in a unique way, you can help students better understand the topic.
Although it is important to pick a popular topic, don't choose to teach a course on how to change a tire if you think that is the stupidest thing anyone could ever do and you would never change your own tire. You might know a lot about the topic, but if you aren't passionate about it and teaching others about it, then the course will fall flat.
It wasn't much fun was it?
You can research topics through Google Keyword and SEORush to get an idea of what people are most interested in learning more about. Step # 2: Pick a Platform
You can choose one that has a built-in audience, such as Udemy or even choose software to install on your site. For example, Forbes reports that the average Udemy instructor makes about $7,000 per course, but the range is wide.
Although it would be impossible to list every option, I'll explore some of the more popular ones available.
Moodle is an Open Source software that you can install on your website to offer online courses through. It allows you to admit certain students, set up password protected folders, and create courses, add videos, and have chat rooms all in one convenient location.
If you want to offer your courses as webinars that users can download on demand, WizIQ is a great option. You can add slides, share info from your desktop, and include audio and video files.
Once you've chosen a platform, it will be easier to write your course, because you'll know what format you need to create the course in. For example, if you're planning to offer a video course, then you'll need to write a script to follow and practice recording lessons.
The person taking the course doesn't know what you know. He or she is a beginner, so start at the beginning.
Enlist a beta group to view your course and let you know if you have any holes of information that need to be filled in.
Read the entire course out loud or have a reading program read it to you. This will help you hear any issues with the wording and catch any awkward phrases or weird typos.
What is going to make yours unique? Why will people want to sign up for your course over the others?
Interviews with other experts
You can build the best course anyone ever created, but if no one takes the course it won't do you much good. Marketing your online course is absolutely essential.
Post articles on your blog that are related to your course, but don't give the content in the course away.
You'll also want to think outside the box.
Another idea is to attend conferences where you can network with potential student.
Once you sell a course to your first students, you'll want to provide the most excellent experience they've ever encountered in an online course. This is what will encourage them to tell their family and friends about your course.
Set up reminders along the way so the student dosn't get distracted and forget to complete the course.
Step # 8: Finding New Topics from Questions
Step # 9: The Power of Residual Income
Online courses are somewhat similar. Once you've created the course, it will continue to bring in money over time.
Residual income is vital to the strength of your business. You can only write courses so fast, but because a course will earn money even long after you've finished writing it, you can exponentially increase your income by writing a second course while the first one is offered, and then a third course, and a fourth and so on.
Step # 10: Other Ways to Monetize Your Courses
I offered courses both online and in-person. At first, I just taught the courses, but then I started to offer some additional add-on services, such as one-on-one tutoring, help with college admission essays, and coaching students through novel writing.
You can easily solve problems in your course by addressing any issues that students bring up. Perhaps there is too much work outside the classroom, the video lessons aren't loading quickly, or parts of the course are confusing.
Ask family and friends to tell others about your courses.
Build a street team to help you promote your course. Offer them a free course if they'll talk it up.
As your reputation is developed, you'll sell more and more courses, creating that residual income we talked about above. In the second part of this article, we'll look at some tips from others who've created successful online courses.
TMDHosting is not perfect but I do recommend them for bloggers or business in need of a reliable web hosting solution. Not only do they offer stable server performances and tons of useful features, but they also have some of the best customer support team in the industry.
TMDhosting has been around for over 10 years and has been considered as a reliable choice for those in need of a quality web hosting provider.
The 100% cloud-based hosting plan allows for great flexibility, giving you room to scale up if your website grows bigger. In terms of resources, you can get as much 200GB of SSD Space and 10TB Bandwidth on the highest tier.
If you’re using WordPress, TMDHosting offers cheap Managed WordPress Hosting service that’s optimized for the platform. Aside from the standard features such as free domain, SSL certificates, and NGINX web server, the WordPress hosting plan is pre-configured to give maximum performance for WordPress websites.
Separated into four tiers, you can either get the Starter, The Original, Smart, and Super Powerful plan. In addition to features such as premium support and unlimited bandwidth, you can get storage from 1TB up to 2x2TB and as high as 32GB DDR4 RAM.
The Cloud solutions we offer are suitable for medium sized websites, they use lots of servers to produce a large “cloud” of computing power. In this cloud, a virtual container is created and this container is similar to a VPS, with the difference that it is quite harder for it to go down, mainly due to the structure of the computing cloud itself.” – TMDHosting Sales Agent, Todd Carter
After testing out TMDHosting, we found that there’s a lot to love about the hosting provider. Here are some of the pros that stand out.
In terms of server performances, TMDHosting can go toe-to-toe with some of the best in the industry. Not only do they strong uptime rates, but they also have blazing fast speed with fast server response time.
TMDHosting recently revamped their Portal Dashboard and made it much more accessible to its users. Now you can manage everything in one convenient portal which houses the billing, support tickets, cPanel login, and other upgrades.
Other companies tend to be quite vague with server limitations, which can be annoying. TMDHosting, on the other hand, will allocate specific CPU seconds per month to each shared hosting account and send alerts to users if they exceed 70% of their CPU seconds.
The Company will notify the Customer in case that their account reaches 70% of their monthly CPU time allotted, in order to work together and find a solution/evaluate the needs of The Customer and/or the software used by the Customer. In cases where the Customer is not taking actions to address CPU time usage over 70% of their monthly plan allotment, the Company reserves the right to limit access to shared CPU resources for the given account until their monthly quota is reset.
The industry standard for money back guarantees on shared and cloud hosting plans are usually within 30 days. TMDHosting, on the other hand, offers a 60-day money back guarantee for their shared and cloud hosting plans.
TMDHosting tends to hold big discounts for new customers. You can get as much as 65% worth of discounts for their different hosting plans if you’re a new customer.
If you tend to focus on a particular continent (i.e. Asia, Europe, or the U.S.), TMD Hosting offers multiple hosting locations that you can choose in order to have better server performances for your targeted audience.
My experience with their customer support team has been excellent. Whether it’s their 24×7 live chat team, forum, and their phone support, I’ve managed to consistently receive fast responses.
1- Auto Backup Feature Can Be Better
If you’re considering the Shared Hosting plan, I’d recommend you go for the Business Plan tier as the long term costs is more or less the same ($8.95/mo vs $9.95/mo) but you’ll have much better server performance and capacity.
Server Usage Limitation Very clear guidelines given. Alert will be sent when users exceed 70% of their CPU seconds.
