If you are answering in writing, proofread all of your responses.
If the interview questions are unclear, ask for clarification.
Publish Guest Posts Guest blogging will help you reach a new audience and spread the word about your book.
Make sure to do your research! Find bloggers with a similar target audience as your own, and read their guest posting guidelines carefully and before coming up with a unique pitch that fits their blog.
Set Up a Virtual Blog Tour Setting up a blog tour requires you to find and contact suitable blogs to see if they would be interested in featuring your book.
Giveaways are a win-win-win for you, the blogger, and their readers. Authors can create a buzz by getting people to enter your giveaway and subscribe to your email list.
With an email list authors can keep subscribers updated and engaged throughout the entire writing and publishing process. Be sure to get started building your list before you publish your book!
Bait Your Hook: The Best Freebies for Generating Leads
Reduced Pricing This isn’t the ideal option for everyone, but it can be a powerful tactic if used correctly.
Consider launching with a temporarily discounted price for the first few days after publication to get more sales and build more buzz. Kindle Countdown Deals allows authors to run limited-time discount promotions of their book.
You can run ads with Google Adwords, Facebook, and other social media outlets. The first step to figuring out which ad network will be the most successful for your book is to know and understand who your target audience is.
Facebooks Ads: With Facebook, you’re able to choose the type of people that you want your book to reach, which will make your ad more relevant and bring you the results you are looking for. Similar to Google Adwords, customers are able to set a budget that can be based on a daily limit for one ad or the total amount for a campaign.
Research by Invesp shows that 90% of consumers read online reviews, and 88% of them trust the online reviews as much as personal recommendations. Authors can ask readers who have purchased their book to write a review, which can attract future customers.
Email your list and ask them for a review
Never pay for reviews, and try not to ask your friends and family (it can be pretty obvious to readers when reviews are fake).
