Unlimited Web Hosting: For Real?
“Unlimited Hosting” refers to web hosting offers that come with unlimited disk storage, data transfer and in some cases, unlimited addon domain.
Why would someone pay $79.99 a month for a hosting plan with limited bandwidth when the same provider is offering an unlimited plan at just $5.45/mo?
Example: BlueHost Unlimited Hosting Plans – Unlimited resources but cost cheaper.
Truth is … there's always a limit
This is where servers are built at Interserver – I took this photo when I visited their data center in 2016 (more photos in my InterServer review). Manpower, network cables, computer hardware – everything is limited.
Web hosting companies are doing all they can, including giving out free migration services and free Google Adwords credits, to win new customers. Because “more if often better” in consumers' beliefs, “unlimited hosting plans” became a popular marketing tactics in mid 2000's (and, if I recall this correctly, BlueHost was the very first that started this).
Overselling happens when a hosting company sells more than they have the actual capacity to provide. Large hosting companies normally own incomprehensible amounts of hosting capacity (bandwidth pipes, computer servers, manpower… etc) that would never be exceeded by a single website.
Unlimited hosting works… until you are using too much
Consider reading an ad for a new all-you-can-eat buffet place and heading over there to try it. Once you get there, there is a note saying you have to weigh less than 70kg (154lbs) before you can enter.
The same applies to many unlimited hosting plans – you are welcomed to host unlimited websites and take up unlimited hosting storage and bandwidth as long X or Y conditions are met.
You can get more details of these limitations in my BlueHost, Hostinger, and iPage review.
That is how “unlimited hosting” works.
You can argue that overselling and unlimited hosting plans are somewhat unethical. However, it does not indicate that the said hosting company is outright evil.
Also, going “unlimited” is never an easy business decision for hosting providers.
Hosting companies – even the very best ones, are in the business to make money. BUT there are some may do it with more honesty than others.
With A2 Hosting you get a choice of either hosting one or multiple websites in three unlimited hosting plans – Lite, Swift, and Turbo. A2's Turbo plans step up the game a little bit and open access to their Turbo option which lays claim to perform up to 20x faster.
Learn more in this A2 Hosting review.
GreenGeeks offers more than just unlimited storage and bandwidth, but also Bonneville Environmental Foundation (BEF) certified green hosting. They are very affordable – Lite plan signup at just $2.95/mo and performed well in our server speed test.
InMotion unlimited hosting comes in three flavors – Launch, Power, and Pro with all three plans give “unlimited” storage and data transfer capacity. InMotion Launch allows up to 2 domains per account, while Power and Pro allow 6 and unlimited domains per account.
The truth is, the quality of a hosting deal relies on a number of factors.
To get an unlimited web host you can trust on, try:
Before we publish a host review, we signup to a web host and use uptime and speed tracking tools to gauge their service quality.
Here are some uptime data (tracked using Uptime Robot) we published in the past:
And here are some speed test results (using Bitcatcha) we published in our reviews.
There are several things you can do to overcome these limitations and protect yourself, including:
So, are we clear on the topic unlimited hosting?
Unlimited hosting is impossible; everything is limited in our world.
Overselling is how they can afford to offer such plans at rock bottom prices.
Make sure you look into details such as site uptime, server response speed, after sales service, software support, and so on.
In case you need help, I have written quite a number of comprehensive web host guides (see below) – I believe they are very helpful for first timers.
